Here's a complete, production-grade solution. The core idea: in Spring AI, the underlying storage of messages is handled by the `ChatMemoryRepository`, whose sole responsibility is to store and retrieve messages, while the `ChatMemory` implementation decides which messages to keep and when to remove them. Since Spring AI ships repository implementations for Cassandra, CosmosDB, in-memory, JDBC, MongoDB, and Neo4j — but **not Elasticsearch** — you implement `ChatMemoryRepository` yourself. That interface is also your clean extension point for custom fields.

## 1. Dependencies

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.ai</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-ai-starter-model-openai</artifactId> <!-- or your model -->
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-data-elasticsearch</artifactId>
</dependency>
```

## 2. Custom document with your extra fields

```java
@Document(indexName = "chat-memory")
@Setting(shards = 1, replicas = 1)
public class ChatMessageDocument {

    @Id
    private String id;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String conversationId;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String messageType;          // USER, ASSISTANT, SYSTEM, TOOL

    @Field(type = FieldType.Text)
    private String content;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Integer)
    private int messageOrder;            // preserves conversation ordering

    @Field(type = FieldType.Date, format = DateFormat.date_time)
    private Instant timestamp;

    // ---- YOUR CUSTOM FIELDS ----
    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String userId;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String tenantId;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String channel;              // web, mobile, api...

    @Field(type = FieldType.Keyword)
    private String modelName;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Integer)
    private Integer tokenCount;

    @Field(type = FieldType.Object, enabled = true)
    private Map<String, Object> metadata = new HashMap<>();

    // getters/setters/builder omitted for brevity
}
```

**Why `messageOrder` matters:** Elasticsearch doesn't guarantee insertion order on retrieval, and message order is critical for LLM context. Always sort on it when reading.

## 3. Context holder for per-request custom fields

The repository's `saveAll` only receives `(conversationId, List<Message>)`, so you need a way to inject request-scoped data (userId, tenantId, etc.). A `ThreadLocal`-based context is the cleanest approach that doesn't fight the framework:

```java
public final class ChatContext {

    public record ContextData(String userId, String tenantId, String channel, String modelName) {}

    private static final ThreadLocal<ContextData> CONTEXT = new ThreadLocal<>();

    public static void set(ContextData data) { CONTEXT.set(data); }
    public static ContextData get() { return CONTEXT.get(); }
    public static void clear() { CONTEXT.remove(); }

    private ChatContext() {}
}
```

> If you use reactive/streaming endpoints, be aware advisors may hop threads. In that case, prefer stuffing custom fields into each `Message`'s metadata map (shown in the mapper below, which reads from both sources) or use `TransmittableThreadLocal`/Reactor context.

## 4. Mapper: Spring AI `Message` ↔ your document

```java
@Component
public class ChatMessageMapper {

    public ChatMessageDocument toDocument(String conversationId, Message message, int order) {
        ChatMessageDocument doc = new ChatMessageDocument();
        doc.setId(conversationId + "-" + order);   // deterministic id → idempotent writes
        doc.setConversationId(conversationId);
        doc.setMessageType(message.getMessageType().name());
        doc.setContent(message.getText());
        doc.setMessageOrder(order);
        doc.setTimestamp(Instant.now());

        // carry over any metadata already attached to the message
        Map<String, Object> meta = new HashMap<>(message.getMetadata());
        meta.remove("messageType"); // internal Spring AI key
        doc.setMetadata(meta);

        // enrich with request-scoped custom fields
        ChatContext.ContextData ctx = ChatContext.get();
        if (ctx != null) {
            doc.setUserId(ctx.userId());
            doc.setTenantId(ctx.tenantId());
            doc.setChannel(ctx.channel());
            doc.setModelName(ctx.modelName());
        }
        return doc;
    }

    public Message toMessage(ChatMessageDocument doc) {
        return switch (MessageType.valueOf(doc.getMessageType())) {
            case USER -> UserMessage.builder()
                    .text(doc.getContent())
                    .metadata(doc.getMetadata())
                    .build();
            case ASSISTANT -> new AssistantMessage(doc.getContent(), doc.getMetadata());
            case SYSTEM -> SystemMessage.builder()
                    .text(doc.getContent())
                    .metadata(doc.getMetadata())
                    .build();
            case TOOL -> new ToolResponseMessage(List.of(), doc.getMetadata());
        };
    }
}
```

## 5. The Elasticsearch `ChatMemoryRepository`

The interface contract you must honor: `deleteByConversationId(String)`, `findByConversationId(String)`, `findConversationIds()`, and `saveAll(String, List<Message>)` — where `saveAll` **replaces** all existing messages for the given conversation ID with the provided messages.

```java
@Repository
public class ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository implements ChatMemoryRepository {

    private static final IndexCoordinates INDEX = IndexCoordinates.of("chat-memory");

    private final ElasticsearchOperations operations;
    private final ChatMessageMapper mapper;

    public ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository(ElasticsearchOperations operations,
                                             ChatMessageMapper mapper) {
        this.operations = operations;
        this.mapper = mapper;
    }

    @Override
    public List<String> findConversationIds() {
        Query query = NativeQuery.builder()
                .withAggregation("conversations", Aggregation.of(a -> a
                        .terms(t -> t.field("conversationId").size(10_000))))
                .withMaxResults(0)
                .build();

        SearchHits<ChatMessageDocument> hits =
                operations.search(query, ChatMessageDocument.class, INDEX);

        ElasticsearchAggregations aggs = (ElasticsearchAggregations) hits.getAggregations();
        if (aggs == null) return List.of();

        return aggs.get("conversations").aggregation().getAggregate()
                .sterms().buckets().array().stream()
                .map(b -> b.key().stringValue())
                .toList();
    }

    @Override
    public List<Message> findByConversationId(String conversationId) {
        Assert.hasText(conversationId, "conversationId must not be empty");

        Query query = NativeQuery.builder()
                .withQuery(q -> q.term(t -> t.field("conversationId").value(conversationId)))
                .withSort(Sort.by(Sort.Direction.ASC, "messageOrder"))
                .withMaxResults(1000)
                .build();

        return operations.search(query, ChatMessageDocument.class, INDEX)
                .stream()
                .map(SearchHit::getContent)
                .map(mapper::toMessage)
                .toList();
    }

    @Override
    public void saveAll(String conversationId, List<Message> messages) {
        Assert.hasText(conversationId, "conversationId must not be empty");
        Assert.notNull(messages, "messages must not be null");

        // Contract: replace-all semantics
        deleteByConversationId(conversationId);

        List<IndexQuery> queries = new ArrayList<>(messages.size());
        for (int i = 0; i < messages.size(); i++) {
            ChatMessageDocument doc = mapper.toDocument(conversationId, messages.get(i), i);
            queries.add(new IndexQueryBuilder()
                    .withId(doc.getId())
                    .withObject(doc)
                    .build());
        }
        operations.bulkIndex(queries, INDEX);
        operations.indexOps(INDEX).refresh(); // read-your-writes within a conversation turn
    }

    @Override
    public void deleteByConversationId(String conversationId) {
        Assert.hasText(conversationId, "conversationId must not be empty");
        Query query = NativeQuery.builder()
                .withQuery(q -> q.term(t -> t.field("conversationId").value(conversationId)))
                .build();
        operations.delete(DeleteQuery.builder(query).withRefresh(true).build(),
                ChatMessageDocument.class, INDEX);
    }
}
```

**Robustness notes:**
- **Explicit refresh** after writes is essential here — Elasticsearch is near-real-time by default (~1s refresh), and without it a rapid follow-up message could read stale history.
- **Deterministic IDs** (`conversationId-order`) make the delete+bulk-index replace operation idempotent under retries.
- The delete-then-index pattern implements the interface's replace-all contract, but isn't atomic. For chat memory this is acceptable (worst case: one turn's history is re-written on retry), but avoid concurrent writes to the *same* conversationId — serialize per-conversation if your app allows parallel messages in one conversation.

## 6. Wire it all together

If a repository bean is already configured, Spring AI will use it instead of the default in-memory one, so your `@Repository` slots right in:

```java
@Configuration
public class ChatConfig {

    @Bean
    public ChatMemory chatMemory(ElasticsearchChatMemoryRepository repository) {
        return MessageWindowChatMemory.builder()
                .chatMemoryRepository(repository)
                .maxMessages(20)
                .build();
    }

    @Bean
    public ChatClient chatClient(ChatClient.Builder builder, ChatMemory chatMemory) {
        return builder
                .defaultAdvisors(MessageChatMemoryAdvisor.builder(chatMemory).build())
                .build();
    }
}
```

`MessageWindowChatMemory` maintains a window of messages up to a specified maximum size (default 20); when exceeded, older messages are evicted but system messages are preserved. Note this means the *LLM context window* is capped — and because `saveAll` replaces everything, evicted messages also disappear from Elasticsearch. If you want a **full audit history** plus a bounded LLM window, see the variant in step 8.

## 7. Service layer — setting the custom context

Every chat memory operation is keyed on a conversation ID; the `ChatMemory` and `ChatMemoryRepository` abstractions use this identifier to decide which conversation to read, append to, or delete, and it must always be provided explicitly.

```java
@Service
public class ChatService {

    private final ChatClient chatClient;

    public ChatService(ChatClient chatClient) {
        this.chatClient = chatClient;
    }

    public String chat(String conversationId, String userId, String tenantId, String userText) {
        ChatContext.set(new ChatContext.ContextData(userId, tenantId, "web", "gpt-4o"));
        try {
            return chatClient.prompt()
                    .user(userText)
                    .advisors(a -> a.param(ChatMemory.CONVERSATION_ID, conversationId))
                    .call()
                    .content();
        } finally {
            ChatContext.clear();
        }
    }
}
```

## 8. Variant: full append-only history + windowed memory

If you need every message permanently retained (auditing, analytics on your custom fields), don't rely on `MessageWindowChatMemory`'s replace-all writes. Instead:

1. Keep the setup above for LLM context.
2. Add a **second, append-only index** (`chat-audit`) written by a custom advisor. The advisor chain invokes multiple advisors sequentially ordered by their `getOrder()` values, with lower values executed first:

```java
@Component
public class AuditAdvisor implements CallAdvisor {

    private final ElasticsearchOperations operations;
    private final ChatMessageMapper mapper;

    // constructor...

    @Override
    public ChatClientResponse adviseCall(ChatClientRequest request, CallAdvisorChain chain) {
        ChatClientResponse response = chain.nextCall(request);

        String conversationId = (String) request.context()
                .getOrDefault(ChatMemory.CONVERSATION_ID, ChatMemory.DEFAULT_CONVERSATION_ID);

        // append user message + assistant response to the audit index (never deleted)
        // ... operations.save(...) into "chat-audit" with your custom fields

        return response;
    }

    @Override
    public String getName() { return "auditAdvisor"; }

    @Override
    public int getOrder() { return Ordered.LOWEST_PRECEDENCE - 100; } // runs after memory advisor
}
```

This gives you the best of both: a bounded, fast conversation window for the model, and an immutable, richly-annotated history you can query/aggregate in Kibana by `userId`, `tenantId`, `tokenCount`, etc.

## Summary of the design

| Concern | Solution |
|---|---|
| ES persistence | Custom `ChatMemoryRepository` (framework auto-detects your bean) |
| Custom fields | `ChatMessageDocument` + `ChatContext` holder + message metadata |
| Ordering | Explicit `messageOrder` field, sorted on read |
| Consistency | Explicit index refresh, deterministic doc IDs |
| Context window | `MessageWindowChatMemory(maxMessages=20)` |
| Full audit trail | Optional append-only advisor to a second index |

One version caveat: Spring AI's message constructors/builders changed slightly between 1.0.x and 1.1.x (e.g., `UserMessage.builder()` availability), so adjust the mapper to your exact version — the repository interface itself is stable since 1.0.0.
